@* The row of links to §11.4's administration surfaces, rendered once instead of copy-pasted six times
   (TECHNICAL_SPECIFICATION §11.4, §11.12).

   Every page renders every link, including its own, marked `aria-current="page"`. The previous
   arrangement had each page omit itself, which made the row a different row on each page — six sets of
   five links, no two the same, and no page reachable from every other. On a handset the row is a
   horizontally scrolled strip (`.page-head-areas`, app.css), and a strip whose contents shift between
   pages cannot be used from memory: the third pill has to be the third pill everywhere.

   A <nav> with a list inside it, because it is navigation and because a screen reader announces the
   count — "six items" is the useful thing to hear about a strip that is partly off-screen. Static
   markup: no injection, no lifecycle, nothing to load. *@

<nav class="page-head-nav" aria-label="Administration areas">
    <ul class="page-head-areas">
        @foreach ((AdministrationArea area, string path, string label) in Areas)
        {
            <li>
                <a href="@path" aria-current="@(area == Current ? "page" : null)">@label</a>
            </li>
        }
    </ul>
</nav>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The set, in the order §11.4 lists the sections. One place, so a seventh surface is one row here
    /// rather than an edit to six pages, five of which somebody will find later.
    /// </summary>
    private static readonly (AdministrationArea Area, string Path, string Label)[] Areas =
    [
        (AdministrationArea.People, "/administration", "People"),
        (AdministrationArea.Tables, "/administration/tables", "Tables"),
        (AdministrationArea.Menu, "/administration/menu", "Menu"),
        (AdministrationArea.Sittings, "/administration/sittings", "Sittings"),
        (AdministrationArea.HiddenRecords, "/administration/hidden-records", "Hidden records"),
        (AdministrationArea.Events, "/administration/events", "Events"),
    ];

    /// <summary>
    /// Which surface is rendering this. Required rather than defaulted: the default would be
    /// <see cref="AdministrationArea.People"/>, and a page that forgot to say would then claim to be the
    /// people page — a highlight that is wrong is worse than one that is missing.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public AdministrationArea Current { get; set; }
}
